🌿 SPRIG ROADMAP: AFTER PLANT COMPARISON
1. Visual Clickable Calendar & Planning

Build Sprig's garden calendar into a genuinely visual, interactive part of the garden story rather than simply another list of dates.

The calendar should bring together the dates Sprig already knows and the things the gardener is planning, eventually allowing the gardener to see and move through:

sowing and planting dates
expected harvest periods
actual harvests
Journal events
garden tasks and reminders
planned sowings and succession planting
important Plant Story dates
other seasonal garden events

The goal is for the gardener to be able to look at the garden through time, move backwards through what happened and forwards through what is planned.

This returns to one of Sprig's original foundations:

✅Journal. Notes. Dates.

2–4. Sprig's Garden Knowledge

These three areas belong closely together.

A large part of the knowledge a gardener accumulates does not belong to a particular Plant Story or Journal event.

At present, this information often ends up scattered through apps such as Google Keep and Notes:

personal gardening notes
information learned through experience
reference information about particular plants
useful guidance gathered through conversations with ChatGPT
techniques worth trying
snippets copied from gardening groups
another gardener's useful observation
links, articles and reference material
things worth remembering next season

Sprig needs a home for this accumulated knowledge without turning it into one enormous miscellaneous Notes drawer.

The three areas below provide different ways into that knowledge.

✅2. Garden Notes / Almanac

The gardener's own evolving notebook of garden knowledge.

This is the natural home for observations, instructions, ideas, seasonal notes, methods and useful information that the gardener wants to keep.

It should feel closer to a well-used garden notebook than a formal reference database.

Over time it can become a personal garden almanac built from the gardener's own climate, garden and experience.

✅3. Plant Reference

Knowledge that belongs specifically to a kind of plant rather than to one individual Plant Story.

For example:

Broccoli

preferred conditions
feeding observations
expected behaviour
harvest patterns
useful growing notes
things learned from previous broccoli Plant Stories
saved knowledge about side shoots, heading, timing, etc.

A Plant Story records:

What happened to this broccoli?

Plant Reference helps answer:

What do I know about growing broccoli?

The two should eventually connect without becoming the same record.

✅4. Saved Tips & Sources

A home for useful knowledge gathered from elsewhere.

Examples:

advice from another gardener in a Facebook gardening group
a useful article
a website
a nursery's growing guide
something learned from ChatGPT
a technique worth trying
a particularly useful comment or explanation
a source the gardener wants to return to

Sprig should preserve where the information came from whenever possible.

A useful piece of gardening knowledge should not become an orphaned sentence whose origin is forgotten six months later.

Garden Notes, Plant Reference and Saved Tips & Sources should ultimately connect with one another rather than becoming three isolated filing cabinets.

The gardener gathers knowledge once. Sprig helps it find the places where it is useful.

5. Garden Gallery

Create a garden-wide visual history using photographs already belonging to records throughout Sprig.

The Gallery should not become a second disconnected photo library.

Instead, it should provide another view into photographs that already belong to:

Plant Stories
Journal entries
Harvests
Growing Places
Garden Trials
and future photographic records

Where dates exist, those dates remain meaningful.

Where source records exist, the gardener should be able to reach them.

One photograph. One piece of garden history. Viewed from another place.

6. Purchases + Costs & Allocations

Develop Sprig's purchase and cost architecture together.

The real-world workflow should remain extremely simple:

“I bought soil today.”

Sprig can then remember the useful detail behind that simple action:

what was bought
quantity
unit
price
supplier
purchase date
price history
Product
what the purchase was eventually used for

Costs & Allocations should allow those purchases to flow naturally into:

Growing Recipes
Plant Stories
Growing Places
harvests
experiments/trials
other appropriate garden records

The gardener should not need to behave like an accountant for Sprig to understand what the garden costs.

7. Supplies + Suppliers

Build the practical garden-resource layer around the purchase system.

Supplies

Understand what the gardener has, uses and may need again.

This can eventually connect Products, Ingredients, Growing Recipes and purchase history without duplicating those records.

Suppliers

Give nurseries, garden centres, landscape suppliers and other sellers their own meaningful records.

Supplier history can eventually answer questions such as:

Where did I buy this?
What did I pay last time?
Which products have I bought there?
Which plants came from this nursery?
Where did that particular growing mix come from?

Purchases, Supplies and Suppliers should form a connected system rather than three separate lists.

🍃 THE THREAD THROUGH THIS ROADMAP

Sprig began with Journal, Notes and Dates.

Plant Comparison has considerably strengthened the Journal/history/evidence side of that original idea.

The next phase returns deliberately to the other two foundations:

✅ Dates → Calendar & Planning

Notes → Garden Notes, Plant Reference and Saved Tips & Sources

Only after that core garden-memory system is substantially established do we move deeper into Gallery, purchasing, costs, supplies and suppliers.

Sprig is not simply recording what is in the garden.

It is gradually becoming the place where the gardener can remember:

what happened, when it happened, what they learned, where that knowledge came from, and what they want to do next.

I think that last distinction is particularly important for our future architecture. Plant Reference shouldn't become Wikipedia-inside-Sprig. The valuable thing is that it can eventually combine reference knowledge with your own accumulated garden knowledge and evidence. That's what makes it different from simply Googling “how to grow broccoli.”

And yes, this corrected roadmap is now the one I'm carrying forward. 🌱

