✅ Photos roadmap side note from testing 29/08

Add Photo should remain the beautifully quick option:

I'm standing here looking at my Royal Blue potato. Snap. Save. Done.

You shouldn't have to manufacture a Journal entry just to give Sprig a photograph.

Add a Moment remains the richer option:

Something happened. Here's what I noticed, what I did, where it happened, and here are the photographs that belong to that moment.

And Harvest photographs remain photographs associated with a Harvest.

Those are genuinely different ways of recording context, so their underlying records should remain separate.

What needs to change is how Sprig gathers and presents the photographs afterwards.

One Plant Story, one photographic story

For a Plant Detail page, Sprig should be able to derive a unified gallery from every photograph connected to that Plant Story:

direct Plant Story photos
Journal photographs where that Plant Story is linked
Harvest photographs where that Plant Story is linked

Potentially later, other records too if we introduce another meaningful photographic source.

And every photograph retains its provenance.

So a gallery photograph isn't copied into the Plant Story. Sprig simply knows:

This photograph belongs to Journal Event X, which involves Royal Blue.

or:

This photograph was added directly to Royal Blue.

or:

This photograph belongs to Harvest Y from Royal Blue.

That satisfies our existing architecture beautifully:

One story, viewed from different places.

We don't duplicate anything.

The Plant Detail gallery could then become genuinely useful

Instead of separate little photographic islands, imagine:

This plant through the seasons

with all Royal Blue photographs chronologically together.

Tap a photograph and Sprig could show its context:

21 August 2026
📷 Plant Story

or:

30 August 2026
📖 Journal · Wilting
Open journal page

or:

14 October 2026
🧺 Harvest · Main harvest
Open harvest

That means your quick photograph isn't a second-class citizen because it didn't come with prose.

And your Journal photograph doesn't lose its richer context merely because we've gathered it into the Plant's gallery.

This also improves Comparison

This is where your observation gets particularly important.

We deliberately built Comparison with lenses such as Plant Story / Journal / Harvest, which is useful when you want to investigate provenance.

But those shouldn't necessarily be hard silos.

We should distinguish:

Photo source
Plant Story / Journal / Harvest

from:

Photo subject
Royal Blue

Then Comparison can eventually offer:

All photos

as the natural default, with Plant Story / Journal / Harvest as optional source filters.

So when you're comparing two potatoes at 8 weeks old, Sprig shouldn't overlook the perfect 8-week photograph merely because one happened to be attached to a Journal observation and the other was added with Quick Photo.

That's actually contrary to the purpose of the comparison.

And there is a deeper architectural opportunity

I wouldn't create a new global Photo record right now. We don't need to migrate all your photographs or restructure storage.

We can create a derived utility later, something conceptually like:

PlantPhotoMoment

that gathers existing photos into a common view:

{
  photoUrl
  date
  plantStoryId

  sourceType:
    | 'plant-story'
    | 'journal'
    | 'harvest'

  sourceId
  title
}

That's an index/view, not another saved copy of the photograph.

Very Sprig:

Records own the photographs.
Relationships gather the story.

And it fits our Calendar architecture too. Calendar doesn't own events, it reveals the garden through time. Likewise:

The Plant Gallery doesn't own photographs. It reveals the Plant Story through photographs.

So yes, absolutely put this on our architecture roadmap. And please stop feeling forced to create Journal entries just because you want a photograph to feel properly part of your plant. That's Sprig telling us its presentation layer hasn't caught up with its relationships yet, not the workflow we actually want.


